 21520, 11, 4565, 8751, 11, 321, 2426, 23218, 8687, 248046], "finish": "eos", "text": "\"From ARPANET to TikTok: How the Internet Rewired Our World\n\nHey there! Ever wonder how we went from sending clunky text emails to streaming 4K videos and doomscrolling through memes? Let’s take a quick trip down memory lane to see how the internet changed everything.\n\nIt all started in the late 1960s with ARPANET, a project funded by the U.S. Department of Defense. Think of it as the internet’s baby steps—just four computers talking to each other to share data. By the 1980s, Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web, giving us the browser and the ability to click links. Suddenly, information wasn’t locked in libraries; it was at our fingertips.\n\nBut here’s the real game-changer for your generation: the shift from desktops to smartphones. In the early 2000s, you needed a computer to get online. Now, you carry the entire internet in your pocket. This mobility has totally reshaped how we socialize, learn, and entertain ourselves.\n\nFor teens, the impact is huge. You’ve grown up with instant access to any fact, song, or video imaginable. It’s made learning easier (shoutout to YouTube tutorials!) but also created new pressures, like the constant need to stay connected and the challenge of filtering through misinformation.\n\nThe internet isn’t just a tool; it’s the environment we live in. It connects us globally, lets us create content, and shapes our culture. So next time you swipe through your feed, remember: you’re riding the wave of a technological revolution that started with a few blinking lights in a lab decades ago. Stay curious, stay critical, and keep exploring!\"", "seconds": 26.82, "fast": false, "mem": {"start": 26.594, "end": 26.712, "max": 27.189, "life": 27.472, "paused_s": 0.0, "frozen_s": 0.0}, "ref2": {"n": 360, "calls": {"qmm16c": 64979, "mlx16": 107700, "linear": 718, "hc_read": 34464, "hc_eager16": 359, "attention": 4308, "experts": 0, "slot_calls": 0, 