powers. His early reign was marked by the need to secure his borders and maintain the fragile peace in the Crusader states. He grew up amidst constant warfare and political maneuvering which shaped his pragmatic approach to governance and diplomacy. The loss of his father meant that he had to navigate complex feudal obligations while still a minor. His guardianship ended when he reached majority and he assumed full control over the County of Tripoli. This transition required him to quickly learn the intricacies of ruling a frontier state that was constantly under pressure from Zengid and later Ayyubid forces.\n\n**Military Campaigns and Alliances**\n\nRaymond III played a crucial role in the defense of the Crusader states against Saladin. He formed strategic alliances with other Christian leaders and sometimes with Muslim emirs to counter the growing power of the Ayyubid dynasty. His military strategies often focused on defensive tactics and securing key fortifications rather than engaging in large-scale offensive campaigns. He was known for his ability to negotiate truces and maintain stability through diplomacy when military options were limited. The Battle of Hattin in 1187 was a catastrophic defeat for the Crusaders and Raymond was among the few nobles who survived the battle. He retreated to Tripoli and worked to rebuild defenses while coordinating with other surviving leaders. His actions during this period were critical in preventing the complete collapse of Christian holdings in the region. He managed to hold onto Tripoli and other territories through careful resource management and strategic withdrawals.\n\n**Later Years and Legacy**\n\nIn his later years Raymond III faced increasing pressure from Saladin’s forces and internal political challenges within the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He served as regent for the young King Baldwin V and later for Baldwin VI. His tenure as regent was marked by efforts to maintain unity among the fractious Crusader nobility. He died in 1187 shortly after the fall of Jerusalem to Saladin. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli and the broader Crusader presence in the Levant. Historians view him as a capable ruler who navigated an impossible situation with skill and resilience. His legacy includes the preservation of Tripoli as a Christian stronghold for several more decades. The political structures he helped maintain allowed for continued resistance against Muslim advances. His life story reflects the broader struggles of the Crusader states to survive in a hostile environment. The end of his rule signaled a shift in power dynamics that would eventually lead to the loss of most Crusader territories.", "seconds": 41.82, "fast": false, "mem": {"start": 26.654, "end": 26.679, "max": 27.553, "life": 27.595, "paused_s": 0.0, "frozen_s": 0.0}, "ref2": {"n": 600, "calls": {"qmm16c": 108419, "mlx16": 179700, "linear": 1198, "hc_read": 57504, "hc_eager16": 599, "attention": 7188, "experts": 0, "slot_calls": 0, "sdpa_split": 360, "kb_pad": 2304, "tier_calls": 0, "gdnx": 21564, "nax4": 0, "att_single": 0, "gdn_inproj": 0, "att_rope": 0, "idx_prep": 0, "hc_write": 0, "gdn_inproj_x": 0, "nax6": 28752, "nax6_ooo": 0, "gdnb": 0, "hc_b": 0, "nax_hybrid": 0, "nax_native": 28752, "nax_repack": 0, "native_3c": 0, "fatt_verify": 0, "verify_old": 7200, "verify_old_eligible": 7188, "fatt_r8": 0, "dense_native": 0, "dense_native_repack": 0, "hc8_fast": 0, "dense_native_unpack": 0}}}
